One Complete Practical Program
If your examiner says, "Create a Git repository, add a file, commit it, and push it to GitHub," follow this procedure.
Aim
To create a Git repository, track files, commit changes, and push them to GitHub.
Commands to Execute
Run these commands one by one in Windows Command Prompt:
git --version
mkdir GitExperiment
cd GitExperiment
git init
git config --global user.name "Student"
git config --global user.email "student@example.com"
echo Hello GitHub > Student.txt
git status
git add Student.txt
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
Now create a new, empty repository on GitHub. Copy its URL and run:
git remote add origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL
Finally:
git push -u origin main
Open your GitHub repository in the browser and verify that Student.txt appears.
Important: Replace the example username, email, and repository URL with your own details. If GitHub asks for authentication, follow the authentication method configured for your account.
